{% comment %}
  Get Outside · Flatirons — paste into TRMNL's markup editor.
  Values come from the plugin's polling URL:
    plate_url  time_of_day  weather  message  date_long

  DO NOT add a <div class="screen"> around this. TRMNL supplies the page
  wrapper itself — its renderer emits

      <body class="trmnl">
        <div class="screen">            <- TRMNL's
          <div class="view view--full"> <- TRMNL's
            ...our markup goes here...

  so a second .screen here nests one inside the other. Both carry 10px of
  padding, which is what put a 20px white band down the left while the
  right-hand margin went to zero and the last 10px of the photo was cut off.
  That was the "blank space on the left" bug — see DECISIONS.md #14.

  The .view--full below IS kept on purpose. It is harmless when TRMNL also
  supplies one (same width, so it lines up exactly), and it keeps the picture
  from collapsing if TRMNL ever hands us a bare .screen — .layout only gets
  its height from a rule that requires a .view--full ancestor.
{% endcomment %}

<div class="view view--full">

  <div class="layout">
    <div class="scene">
      <img class="image scene__plate"
           src="{{ plate_url }}"
           alt="The Boulder Flatirons, {{ time_of_day }}, {{ weather }}" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="title_bar">
    <span class="title">{{ message }}</span>
    <span class="instance">{{ date_long }}</span>
  </div>

</div>

<style>
  /* Only padding is changed here.

     Do NOT add "display: block" or "margin: 0" to .layout. The picture takes
     its height from TRMNL's flex layout, and its bottom margin is what makes
     the gap above the bar. Setting either one pushed the picture down out of
     sight. Both were tried and both broke it. */
  .layout { padding: 0; }

  .scene {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* "cover" fills the space completely. If the picture and the space are
     slightly different shapes it trims a sliver off the edges, which looks
     better than the blank paper that "contain" leaves.

     No "image-dither" class: the picture already has its dot pattern baked
     in, and letting TRMNL add its own on top makes a muddy mess. */
  .scene__plate {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center;
    image-rendering: pixelated;
  }

  /* TRMNL makes the date grey by default. Grey almost disappears on an e-ink
     screen, so both labels are forced to solid black. */
  .title_bar .title,
  .title_bar .instance {
    color: #000 !important;
  }

  /* TRMNL already leaves 10px around everything. This adds 16px more inside
     the bar so the text isn't jammed against the rounded corner. */
  .title_bar {
    padding-left: 16px !important;
    padding-right: 16px !important;
  }
</style>
